INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2036,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-young-people-climate-rapidly-world.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-young-people-climate-rapidly-world.html','How 39 young people experience climate education in a rapidly changing world','how39youngpeopleexperienceclimateeducationinarapidlychangingworld','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T01:40:01Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Ecological grief is growing—and our current approach to climate change education isn''t keeping pace.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2037,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-africans-reuse-recycle-resell-circular.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-africans-reuse-recycle-resell-circular.html','Africans reuse, repair, recycle and resell waste: What''s needed to build on the circular economy basics','africansreuserepairrecycleandresellwastewhatsneededtobuildonthecirculareconomybasics','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-22T00:00:01Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','In many African cities, a broken fridge is rarely thrown away immediately. It may be repaired and resold, dismantled for spare parts or collected for its metal components.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2038,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-vital-militaries-accountable-environmental-toll.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-vital-militaries-accountable-environmental-toll.html','Data vital in holding militaries accountable for the environmental toll of war','datavitalinholdingmilitariesaccountablefortheenvironmentaltollofwar','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T22:30:06Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Ahead of the U.N. Climate Change Conference (COP31) meeting in Antalya, Turkiye, held in Nov. 2026, an expert writing in The BMJ''s climate issue argues that accurate data on greenhouse gas emissions during armed conflict is urgently needed to hold militaries, governments and individuals accountable for the environmental impact of war.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2039,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-military-worsen-climate-undermine-experts.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-military-worsen-climate-undermine-experts.html','Rising military spending could worsen climate change and undermine security, experts warn','risingmilitaryspendingcouldworsenclimatechangeandunderminesecurityexpertswarn','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T22:30:01Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Investing more in the military is not a solution to climate change and may cost us our security, warn experts in The BMJ''s climate issue. In the first article, Neta Crawford at the University of St Andrews says the view that climate change is a "threat multiplier" that may cause instability and provoke conflict is leading governments to justify higher defense spending.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2040,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-air-quality-modest-impact-montrealers.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-air-quality-modest-impact-montrealers.html','Study finds air quality warnings had only a modest impact on Montrealers'' movements during wildfires','studyfindsairqualitywarningshadonlyamodestimpactonmontrealersmovementsduringwildfires','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T22:00:11Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','When Environment and Climate Change Canada (ECCC) urged Montrealers to limit outdoor activity during Canada''s worst wildfire smoke episodes, the city''s travel patterns shifted—but far less than public health officials might have hoped, according to a new McGill University study published in Health & Place.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2041,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-massive-ice-shelf-buttressed-age.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-massive-ice-shelf-buttressed-age.html','How a massive ice shelf buttressed the last ice age''s glaciers—and what it could mean for Antarctica today','howamassiveiceshelfbuttressedthelasticeagesglaciersandwhatitcouldmeanforantarcticatoday','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T21:20:08Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','About 20,000 years ago, an enormous, floating ice shelf more than a mile (1.6 kilometers) thick helped lock in three of the largest ice sheets in northern Canada and Greenland. When that ice shelf disappeared, it helped unlock part of these ice sheets, which contributed to hundreds of feet of sea level rise, establishing modern coastlines as we know them.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2042,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-el-nio-threatening-california-fragile.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-el-nio-threatening-california-fragile.html','El Niño is threatening California''s fragile coast again—the 1982–83 El Niño opened eyes to the serious erosion risks','elninoisthreateningcaliforniasfragilecoastagainthe198283elninoopenedeyestotheseriouserosionrisks','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T21:00:07Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','California''s coastal bluffs and beaches look rugged, but they can be surprisingly fragile in the face of a powerful El Niño.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2043,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-salt-marshes-powerful-allies-climate.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-salt-marshes-powerful-allies-climate.html','Salt marshes are proving to be powerful allies in building a climate-resilient coastline','saltmarshesareprovingtobepowerfulalliesinbuildingaclimateresilientcoastline','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T20:40:01Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Salt marsh sediment and vegetation can remain largely intact even during extreme storm surges. This was demonstrated in a large-scale experiment in which researchers transported sections of salt marsh to an experimental facility capable of producing the world''s largest artificial waves. The results show how salt marshes can contribute to natural coastal protection, both in the Netherlands and beyond. The results were published in the journals Nature and Communications Sustainability.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2044,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-reconstructing-years-summer-temperature-western.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-reconstructing-years-summer-temperature-western.html','Reconstructing 16,000 years of summer temperature change in the Western Balkans','reconstructing16000yearsofsummertemperaturechangeinthewesternbalkans','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T20:30:02Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Researchers at Eötvös Loránd University (ELTE) have reconstructed approximately 16,000 years of summer temperature changes from sediments collected at Lake Prokoško in Bosnia and Herzegovina. The findings show how the Western Balkans responded to rapid climate changes at the end of the last glacial period, how warmer and cooler phases unfolded during the Holocene, and how the impact of human land use became increasingly pronounced.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2045,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-subsidence-drought-ground-collapse-homes.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-subsidence-drought-ground-collapse-homes.html','Subsidence claims: After drought, ground can shrink, swell or even collapse when it rains—causing damage to homes','subsidenceclaimsafterdroughtgroundcanshrinkswellorevencollapsewhenitrainscausingdamagetohomes','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T19:20:16Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','A crack appears beside a window after weeks of scorching weather. As much as you might hope the ground beneath your house will recover with the first rain, the effects of a dry summer can continue long after temperatures fall.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2046,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-methane-emissions-baltimore-seasonal-daily.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-methane-emissions-baltimore-seasonal-daily.html','Methane emissions in Baltimore show seasonal, daily trends','methaneemissionsinbaltimoreshowseasonaldailytrends','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T17:20:17Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Natural gas leaks are a significant source of methane emissions in cities, but it can be hard to pinpoint exactly where and why they happen. And methane, a potent greenhouse gas that is the main chemical component of natural gas, has been historically undercounted or omitted in emissions estimates, according to a team of atmospheric and climate science researchers in the Penn State College of Earth and Mineral Sciences.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2047,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-life-earth-climate.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-life-earth-climate.html','How life on Earth experiences climate change','howlifeonearthexperiencesclimatechange','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T16:20:23Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Climate change can look very different at the scale of a forest, field or hillside. A new review shows how changes in vegetation and atmospheric conditions can cause local microclimates to warm at very different rates from the regional climate—and why this matters for understanding how biodiversity will respond to climate change.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2048,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-world-ground-curb-climate-faulting.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-world-ground-curb-climate-faulting.html','UN projections say world is losing ground in fight to curb climate change, faulting US a bit','unprojectionssayworldislosinggroundinfighttocurbclimatechangefaultingusabit','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T15:20:25Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Losing more ground in the fight against climate change, humanity is heading for a hotter world than experts projected last year—and the United States is a big reason for that, a new United Nations report says.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2049,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-technology-future-extremes-peak.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-technology-future-extremes-peak.html','How climate change, new technology are shaping the future of extremes in peak power demands','howclimatechangenewtechnologyareshapingthefutureofextremesinpeakpowerdemands','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T14:40:14Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','New research led by the University of Michigan provides utilities and policymakers with forecasts that can help them better prepare for future extremes in peak energy demand in the United States.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2050,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-reveals-climate-nz-extreme.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-reveals-climate-nz-extreme.html','AI reveals how a warming climate will intensify NZ''s most extreme downpours','airevealshowawarmingclimatewillintensifynzsmostextremedownpours','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T13:00:25Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','The most damaging weather events are the record-breaking "one-in-100-year" disasters communities have usually only seen once.',4.0,'classified',1932,20);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2051,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-tackling-lake-erie-algal-blooms.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-tackling-lake-erie-algal-blooms.html','Tackling Lake Erie''s algal blooms requires targeted conservation strategies','tacklinglakeeriesalgalbloomsrequirestargetedconservationstrategies','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T12:40:01Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Anyone living along the coast of Lake Erie will be well aware of the periodic algal blooms that appear on its surface. The greenish blooms can cause rashes and other health problems and pose risks to both humans and animals.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(2052,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-smart-outdoor-sports-tournaments-environmental.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-smart-outdoor-sports-tournaments-environmental.html','Smart planning of outdoor sports tournaments using environmental heat measurements','smartplanningofoutdoorsportstournamentsusingenvironmentalheatmeasurements','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-21T11:40:04Z','2026-10-22T03:01:37Z','Rising temperatures caused by climate change are making it harder to organize outdoor sports events safely. Exercising in the heat lowers performance and raises the risk of heatstroke, which is a leading cause of nontraumatic death among professional athletes.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
